1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,538
Sweetheart, I have a shoebox in my

2
00:00:02,538 --> 00:00:05,673
closet with every Christmas card my mother

3
00:00:05,673 --> 00:00:06,420
ever kept.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,322
Over four hundred of them,

5
00:00:08,322 --> 00:00:11,569
from nineteen forty-six to nineteen ninety-seven.

6
00:00:11,749 --> 00:00:13,844
And here's what I noticed when I

7
00:00:13,844 --> 00:00:15,023
went through them.

8
00:00:15,203 --> 00:00:18,182
She threw away the store-bought ones with

9
00:00:18,182 --> 00:00:19,417
just a signature.

10
00:00:19,597 --> 00:00:22,794
She kept every single one where somebody

11
00:00:22,794 --> 00:00:25,111
had written a few real words,

12
00:00:25,111 --> 00:00:25,751
by hand.

13
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,918
So tonight I'm giving you seven Christmas

14
00:00:28,918 --> 00:00:31,805
card mistakes that send your card straight

15
00:00:31,805 --> 00:00:32,698
to the trash,

16
00:00:32,698 --> 00:00:34,897
and how to write one that people

17
00:00:34,897 --> 00:00:36,340
keep for fifty years.

18
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:37,879
And stay with me,

19
00:00:37,879 --> 00:00:40,436
because the last one is the card

20
00:00:40,436 --> 00:00:41,874
that matters most,

21
00:00:41,874 --> 00:00:44,751
and it's the one almost nobody sends

22
00:00:44,751 --> 00:00:45,391
anymore.

23
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:47,930
In our house on Elm Street,

24
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:50,605
Christmas cards were a big event.

25
00:00:50,785 --> 00:00:52,848
My mother bought a box of fifty

26
00:00:52,848 --> 00:00:55,643
at Woolworth's the day after Thanksgiving,

27
00:00:55,643 --> 00:00:57,839
and she wrote them at the kitchen

28
00:00:57,839 --> 00:00:59,902
table every evening for a week,

29
00:00:59,902 --> 00:01:01,698
with her good fountain pen.

30
00:01:01,878 --> 00:01:04,084
Every card got a real note.

31
00:01:04,264 --> 00:01:06,005
And when the cards came in,

32
00:01:06,005 --> 00:01:08,326
she taped them around the doorway to

33
00:01:08,326 --> 00:01:09,293
the front room,

34
00:01:09,293 --> 00:01:11,613
so the door was framed in Christmas.

35
00:01:11,963 --> 00:01:14,322
The mailman came twice a day in

36
00:01:14,322 --> 00:01:15,768
December back then,

37
00:01:15,768 --> 00:01:16,224
honey.

38
00:01:16,404 --> 00:01:18,343
Morning and afternoon.

39
00:01:18,523 --> 00:01:20,596
Danny and I ran to the mailbox

40
00:01:20,596 --> 00:01:22,945
every time, and we fought over who

41
00:01:22,945 --> 00:01:24,119
got to open them.

42
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:26,540
Getting a card meant somebody,

43
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:29,454
somewhere, sat down and thought of you.

44
00:01:29,634 --> 00:01:31,576
In a minute I'll tell you the

45
00:01:31,576 --> 00:01:33,987
first mistake, and it's the one that

46
00:01:33,987 --> 00:01:35,996
makes a card feel like a bill.

47
00:01:36,346 --> 00:01:37,856
Mistake number one.

48
00:01:38,036 --> 00:01:39,510
Just a signature.

49
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:42,945
A printed card that says Merry Christmas,

50
00:01:42,945 --> 00:01:44,612
signed Bob and Linda,

51
00:01:44,612 --> 00:01:46,041
with nothing else.

52
00:01:46,221 --> 00:01:48,636
It takes three seconds to read and

53
00:01:48,636 --> 00:01:50,412
one second to throw away.

54
00:01:50,592 --> 00:01:52,078
The fix is simple.

55
00:01:52,258 --> 00:01:55,275
Write at least two or three sentences

56
00:01:55,275 --> 00:01:55,927
by hand.

57
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:57,187
That's all.

58
00:01:57,367 --> 00:01:58,551
Not a letter.

59
00:01:58,731 --> 00:02:01,322
Just a few real words for that

60
00:02:01,322 --> 00:02:02,272
one person.

61
00:02:02,621 --> 00:02:04,162
Here's my rule,

62
00:02:04,162 --> 00:02:05,292
sweetheart.

63
00:02:05,472 --> 00:02:08,518
Every card gets one memory and one

64
00:02:08,518 --> 00:02:08,966
wish.

65
00:02:09,146 --> 00:02:10,342
One memory.

66
00:02:10,522 --> 00:02:12,854
I still think about the Christmas Eve

67
00:02:12,854 --> 00:02:14,808
we sang carols on your porch in

68
00:02:14,808 --> 00:02:16,258
nineteen seventy-eight.

69
00:02:16,438 --> 00:02:17,924
And one wish.

70
00:02:18,104 --> 00:02:19,938
I hope your new knee lets you

71
00:02:19,938 --> 00:02:22,341
dance at your granddaughter's wedding.

72
00:02:22,521 --> 00:02:24,176
That's three sentences,

73
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:26,478
and the person will read it four

74
00:02:26,478 --> 00:02:26,910
times.

75
00:02:27,259 --> 00:02:28,908
Mistake number two.

76
00:02:29,088 --> 00:02:31,834
The long typed newsletter all about how

77
00:02:31,834 --> 00:02:33,523
wonderful your year was.

78
00:02:33,703 --> 00:02:35,992
Johnny made the honor roll,

79
00:02:35,992 --> 00:02:38,705
Susie won the piano competition,

80
00:02:38,705 --> 00:02:41,248
and we took a lovely cruise to

81
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:41,842
Alaska.

82
00:02:42,022 --> 00:02:44,303
People call them brag letters,

83
00:02:44,303 --> 00:02:47,040
honey, and they groan when they open

84
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:47,420
them.

85
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,240
If you send a letter,

86
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:50,802
keep it to one page,

87
00:02:50,802 --> 00:02:53,769
be honest, include the funny parts and

88
00:02:53,769 --> 00:02:54,940
the hard parts,

89
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:56,424
and ask about them.

90
00:02:56,774 --> 00:02:59,413
My friend Joyce sent the best Christmas

91
00:02:59,413 --> 00:03:00,699
letter I ever read,

92
00:03:00,699 --> 00:03:02,323
in nineteen eighty-four.

93
00:03:02,503 --> 00:03:05,314
It said, this year the roof leaked,

94
00:03:05,314 --> 00:03:08,205
the dog ate the Thanksgiving turkey,

95
00:03:08,205 --> 00:03:10,694
Harold had his gallbladder out,

96
00:03:10,694 --> 00:03:13,344
and we have never been happier to

97
00:03:13,344 --> 00:03:14,067
be alive.

98
00:03:14,247 --> 00:03:15,466
We miss you.

99
00:03:15,646 --> 00:03:16,714
Come visit.

100
00:03:16,894 --> 00:03:20,168
Everybody on her list kept that letter.

101
00:03:20,348 --> 00:03:22,197
I read it every December,

102
00:03:22,197 --> 00:03:23,529
and I still laugh.

103
00:03:23,879 --> 00:03:25,597
Mistake number three.

104
00:03:25,777 --> 00:03:29,115
Printed address labels and a printed return

105
00:03:29,115 --> 00:03:29,736
address.

106
00:03:29,916 --> 00:03:31,916
It looks like a bill or an

107
00:03:31,916 --> 00:03:32,992
advertisement.

108
00:03:33,172 --> 00:03:35,402
People open the bills last.

109
00:03:35,582 --> 00:03:37,834
Address the envelope by hand.

110
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:39,771
And use a real stamp,

111
00:03:39,771 --> 00:03:42,196
a Christmas stamp if you can,

112
00:03:42,196 --> 00:03:44,539
not a machine postage strip.

113
00:03:44,719 --> 00:03:47,880
An envelope with handwriting and a pretty

114
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:50,501
stamp is the first one people open

115
00:03:50,501 --> 00:03:52,660
from the whole pile of mail.

116
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:54,682
If this is helping you,

117
00:03:54,682 --> 00:03:57,154
honey, tap the like button for me.

118
00:03:57,334 --> 00:04:00,028
It helps Grandma more than you know.

119
00:04:00,208 --> 00:04:02,808
And a little trick my mother used.

120
00:04:02,988 --> 00:04:05,837
Tuck something small and flat inside the

121
00:04:05,837 --> 00:04:06,193
card.

122
00:04:06,373 --> 00:04:08,614
A pressed sprig of pine.

123
00:04:08,794 --> 00:04:10,605
A paper snowflake.

124
00:04:10,785 --> 00:04:12,271
A little photo.

125
00:04:12,451 --> 00:04:14,123
A recipe card.

126
00:04:14,303 --> 00:04:15,847
A child's drawing.

127
00:04:16,027 --> 00:04:18,736
It makes opening it feel like opening

128
00:04:18,736 --> 00:04:19,835
a tiny present.

129
00:04:20,185 --> 00:04:21,787
Mistake number four.

130
00:04:21,967 --> 00:04:23,575
Sending them too late,

131
00:04:23,575 --> 00:04:25,037
on the twenty-third,

132
00:04:25,037 --> 00:04:27,303
so they arrive after Christmas,

133
00:04:27,303 --> 00:04:30,593
with the bills and the after-Christmas sales.

134
00:04:30,773 --> 00:04:32,883
Mail your cards in the first two

135
00:04:32,883 --> 00:04:34,070
weeks of December.

136
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:36,866
That way, people have time to enjoy

137
00:04:36,866 --> 00:04:39,183
them, hang them up and set them

138
00:04:39,183 --> 00:04:40,229
on the mantel.

139
00:04:40,409 --> 00:04:43,675
And send them early to anybody overseas,

140
00:04:43,675 --> 00:04:45,553
by the end of November.

141
00:04:45,903 --> 00:04:48,847
So far, write a few real words,

142
00:04:48,847 --> 00:04:50,841
skip the brag letter,

143
00:04:50,841 --> 00:04:52,645
address it by hand,

144
00:04:52,645 --> 00:04:54,355
and mail it early.

145
00:04:54,535 --> 00:04:56,968
And here's how to make it easy,

146
00:04:56,968 --> 00:04:57,832
sweetheart.

147
00:04:58,012 --> 00:04:59,685
Do five cards a night,

148
00:04:59,685 --> 00:05:01,131
like my mother did,

149
00:05:01,131 --> 00:05:03,108
with a cup of tea and some

150
00:05:03,108 --> 00:05:04,630
carols on the radio.

151
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:07,735
Fifty cards is ten quiet evenings.

152
00:05:07,915 --> 00:05:10,003
It's one of the nicest parts of

153
00:05:10,003 --> 00:05:12,158
December, if you don't try to do

154
00:05:12,158 --> 00:05:13,302
them all at once.

155
00:05:13,653 --> 00:05:15,215
And keep a list,

156
00:05:15,215 --> 00:05:15,800
honey.

157
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:17,440
A little notebook,

158
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:19,955
or a page in your address book,

159
00:05:19,955 --> 00:05:21,253
with every name,

160
00:05:21,253 --> 00:05:23,930
the address, and a check mark for

161
00:05:23,930 --> 00:05:26,282
the years you sent a card and

162
00:05:26,282 --> 00:05:28,066
the years you got one.

163
00:05:28,246 --> 00:05:31,361
My mother's list goes back to nineteen

164
00:05:31,361 --> 00:05:33,082
forty-six, in pencil,

165
00:05:33,082 --> 00:05:36,114
with names crossed out and new babies

166
00:05:36,114 --> 00:05:36,606
added.

167
00:05:36,786 --> 00:05:39,463
It's the history of our whole family,

168
00:05:39,463 --> 00:05:40,837
in one little book.

169
00:05:41,017 --> 00:05:42,468
When someone moves,

170
00:05:42,468 --> 00:05:44,988
write the new address right away.

171
00:05:45,338 --> 00:05:47,056
Mistake number five.

172
00:05:47,236 --> 00:05:50,150
The exact same message in every card.

173
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:52,338
People compare, honey.

174
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:55,082
Your sister and your aunt will put

175
00:05:55,082 --> 00:05:57,495
them side by side on the kitchen

176
00:05:57,495 --> 00:05:58,927
counter and notice.

177
00:05:59,107 --> 00:06:01,921
Write something different for each person,

178
00:06:01,921 --> 00:06:03,194
even if it's short.

179
00:06:03,374 --> 00:06:04,927
Mention their garden,

180
00:06:04,927 --> 00:06:06,407
their new grandbaby,

181
00:06:06,407 --> 00:06:08,922
their dog, the pie they brought to

182
00:06:08,922 --> 00:06:09,736
the picnic.

183
00:06:09,916 --> 00:06:13,004
Specific is what makes it feel personal.

184
00:06:13,355 --> 00:06:15,096
Mistake number six.

185
00:06:15,276 --> 00:06:18,095
A photo card with nothing written on

186
00:06:18,095 --> 00:06:18,329
it.

187
00:06:18,509 --> 00:06:21,168
Family photo cards are lovely.

188
00:06:21,348 --> 00:06:24,425
I keep every one of my grandchildren's.

189
00:06:24,605 --> 00:06:26,759
But a photo with only a printed

190
00:06:26,759 --> 00:06:28,983
greeting is like a smile with no

191
00:06:28,983 --> 00:06:29,400
hello.

192
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:31,560
Turn it over and write a few

193
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:33,399
words on the back by hand.

194
00:06:33,579 --> 00:06:35,054
Even one line.

195
00:06:35,234 --> 00:06:36,287
Thinking of you,

196
00:06:36,287 --> 00:06:38,984
Aunt Ruth, the twins are finally sleeping

197
00:06:38,984 --> 00:06:40,168
through the night.

198
00:06:40,518 --> 00:06:41,662
And date it,

199
00:06:41,662 --> 00:06:42,712
sweetheart.

200
00:06:42,892 --> 00:06:44,966
Write the year on the back of

201
00:06:44,966 --> 00:06:46,826
every photo card you keep,

202
00:06:46,826 --> 00:06:47,327
in pen.

203
00:06:47,507 --> 00:06:48,553
In forty years,

204
00:06:48,553 --> 00:06:51,484
your grandchildren won't know if that baby

205
00:06:51,484 --> 00:06:53,717
in the Santa hat is their father

206
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:54,763
or their uncle.

207
00:06:54,943 --> 00:06:57,196
My mother wrote the names and the

208
00:06:57,196 --> 00:06:58,902
year on every photo card.

209
00:06:59,082 --> 00:07:00,203
Thanks to her,

210
00:07:00,203 --> 00:07:02,924
I know exactly who everybody is in

211
00:07:02,924 --> 00:07:05,166
a box of four hundred cards.

212
00:07:05,516 --> 00:07:07,803
And now mistake number seven,

213
00:07:07,803 --> 00:07:09,932
the card that matters most,

214
00:07:09,932 --> 00:07:12,772
the one almost nobody sends anymore.

215
00:07:12,952 --> 00:07:15,216
Forgetting the people who are alone.

216
00:07:15,396 --> 00:07:17,149
The widow down the street.

217
00:07:17,329 --> 00:07:18,641
The old teacher.

218
00:07:18,821 --> 00:07:21,527
Your mother's old friend in the nursing

219
00:07:21,527 --> 00:07:21,874
home.

220
00:07:22,054 --> 00:07:24,678
The neighbor whose husband died this year.

221
00:07:24,858 --> 00:07:27,393
For them, a Christmas card might be

222
00:07:27,393 --> 00:07:30,001
the only mail they get all December.

223
00:07:30,352 --> 00:07:32,081
When my husband passed,

224
00:07:32,081 --> 00:07:33,810
in two thousand eleven,

225
00:07:33,810 --> 00:07:35,539
I got a card from a boy

226
00:07:35,539 --> 00:07:38,470
I'd taught in Sunday school in nineteen

227
00:07:38,470 --> 00:07:39,373
seventy-two.

228
00:07:39,553 --> 00:07:41,991
He was sixty years old by then.

229
00:07:42,171 --> 00:07:44,272
He wrote, Mrs.

230
00:07:44,452 --> 00:07:47,275
Ruth, you taught me the Christmas story

231
00:07:47,275 --> 00:07:49,447
with a flannel board and a box

232
00:07:49,447 --> 00:07:50,823
of animal crackers,

233
00:07:50,823 --> 00:07:52,705
and I've told it to my own

234
00:07:52,705 --> 00:07:54,588
children every year since.

235
00:07:54,768 --> 00:07:57,393
I put that card on my nightstand,

236
00:07:57,393 --> 00:07:59,063
and it's still there.

237
00:07:59,414 --> 00:08:00,351
So this year,

238
00:08:00,351 --> 00:08:02,875
honey, add five people to your list

239
00:08:02,875 --> 00:08:05,544
who aren't expecting a card from you.

240
00:08:05,724 --> 00:08:06,745
And if you can,

241
00:08:06,745 --> 00:08:09,265
write something they'll want to keep.

242
00:08:09,445 --> 00:08:11,488
Tell them what they meant to you.

243
00:08:11,668 --> 00:08:14,271
People wait their whole lives to hear

244
00:08:14,271 --> 00:08:16,382
it, and most of them never do.

245
00:08:16,562 --> 00:08:18,654
A card is the easiest way in

246
00:08:18,654 --> 00:08:20,149
the world to say it.

247
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,589
And don't forget the children,

248
00:08:22,589 --> 00:08:23,356
sweetheart.

249
00:08:23,536 --> 00:08:26,485
Children almost never get real mail.

250
00:08:26,665 --> 00:08:28,930
Send a card to each grandchild,

251
00:08:28,930 --> 00:08:30,172
niece and nephew,

252
00:08:30,172 --> 00:08:32,073
addressed to them by name,

253
00:08:32,073 --> 00:08:33,607
not to their parents.

254
00:08:33,787 --> 00:08:35,800
Put a dollar bill or a sheet

255
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:37,166
of stickers inside.

256
00:08:37,346 --> 00:08:39,952
Danny's grandson told me my card was

257
00:08:39,952 --> 00:08:42,052
the only mail he got all year

258
00:08:42,052 --> 00:08:43,789
with his own name on it.

259
00:08:43,969 --> 00:08:46,504
He's twelve, and he answered with a

260
00:08:46,504 --> 00:08:47,011
letter.

261
00:08:47,191 --> 00:08:48,364
In pencil.

262
00:08:48,714 --> 00:08:50,757
And when the cards come in,

263
00:08:50,757 --> 00:08:52,649
display them, sweetheart.

264
00:08:52,829 --> 00:08:55,084
Tape them around a doorway like my

265
00:08:55,084 --> 00:08:55,813
mother did.

266
00:08:55,993 --> 00:08:58,128
Hang them on a ribbon across the

267
00:08:58,128 --> 00:09:00,196
mantel with little clothespins.

268
00:09:00,376 --> 00:09:02,647
Or put them in a big basket

269
00:09:02,647 --> 00:09:03,657
by the sofa,

270
00:09:03,657 --> 00:09:06,518
so visitors can look through them.

271
00:09:06,698 --> 00:09:08,314
And after Christmas,

272
00:09:08,314 --> 00:09:10,740
keep the ones that matter in a

273
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:11,063
box.

274
00:09:11,243 --> 00:09:12,494
In fifty years,

275
00:09:12,494 --> 00:09:14,830
that box will be a treasure.

276
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:16,140
And the rest,

277
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:18,796
honey, cut out the pretty fronts and

278
00:09:18,796 --> 00:09:21,158
use them as gift tags next year,

279
00:09:21,158 --> 00:09:22,855
like I told you before.

280
00:09:23,035 --> 00:09:25,418
Or let the grandchildren cut them up

281
00:09:25,418 --> 00:09:26,146
for crafts.

282
00:09:26,326 --> 00:09:29,018
My mother made a little Christmas card

283
00:09:29,018 --> 00:09:30,576
quilt from the fronts,

284
00:09:30,576 --> 00:09:33,693
glued onto cardboard and laced together with

285
00:09:33,693 --> 00:09:34,047
yarn.

286
00:09:34,227 --> 00:09:36,604
It hung in our kitchen for twenty

287
00:09:36,604 --> 00:09:37,036
years.

288
00:09:37,387 --> 00:09:39,678
And if you're short on money for

289
00:09:39,678 --> 00:09:41,612
cards and stamps this year,

290
00:09:41,612 --> 00:09:43,760
honey, there's no shame in it.

291
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:45,171
Make them.

292
00:09:45,351 --> 00:09:47,604
Fold a piece of plain paper,

293
00:09:47,604 --> 00:09:49,133
draw a little tree,

294
00:09:49,133 --> 00:09:51,144
or let the children draw.

295
00:09:51,324 --> 00:09:53,448
Or send an email or a text

296
00:09:53,448 --> 00:09:56,061
with a real message and a photo.

297
00:09:56,241 --> 00:09:58,029
The rule is the same.

298
00:09:58,209 --> 00:09:59,709
A few real words,

299
00:09:59,709 --> 00:10:01,739
written for one person.

300
00:10:01,919 --> 00:10:03,774
That's what people keep,

301
00:10:03,774 --> 00:10:04,856
not the paper.

302
00:10:05,206 --> 00:10:06,599
So one more time.

303
00:10:06,779 --> 00:10:08,972
Write a few real words,

304
00:10:08,972 --> 00:10:11,260
one memory and one wish.

305
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:13,062
Skip the brag letter,

306
00:10:13,062 --> 00:10:15,225
or make it honest and funny.

307
00:10:15,405 --> 00:10:17,009
Address it by hand,

308
00:10:17,009 --> 00:10:18,528
with a real stamp.

309
00:10:18,708 --> 00:10:20,055
Mail it early.

310
00:10:20,235 --> 00:10:22,986
Write something different for each person.

311
00:10:23,166 --> 00:10:25,418
Write on the back of the photo,

312
00:10:25,418 --> 00:10:26,290
and date it.

313
00:10:26,470 --> 00:10:28,798
And send a card to somebody who's

314
00:10:28,798 --> 00:10:29,221
alone.

315
00:10:29,401 --> 00:10:31,830
That's how to write a Christmas card

316
00:10:31,830 --> 00:10:33,720
people keep for fifty years.

317
00:10:34,070 --> 00:10:35,893
Tell me in the comments,

318
00:10:35,893 --> 00:10:36,728
sweetheart.

319
00:10:36,908 --> 00:10:39,544
Do you still send Christmas cards?

320
00:10:39,724 --> 00:10:42,087
And what's the best one you ever

321
00:10:42,087 --> 00:10:42,382
got?

322
00:10:42,562 --> 00:10:44,432
I'd love to hear about it.

323
00:10:44,612 --> 00:10:48,044
The whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

324
00:10:48,044 --> 00:10:50,283
is right there on your screen,

325
00:10:50,283 --> 00:10:52,372
and I'll see you in the next

326
00:10:52,372 --> 00:10:52,820
video.

327
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,579
Good night, honey.

328
00:10:54,759 --> 00:10:56,198
Merry Christmas.
